# The claude-otel compose stack goes only to machines that have to provide their own
# collector — which is fewer than the machines that report telemetry. Everywhere else the
# files are not merely useless, they are a loaded gun: every one of these hosts already has
# something listening on 4317, or depends on something that does, and `docker compose up`
# here would bind that port out from under it.
#
#   daniel-server, daniel-box   already run an observability stack whose collector holds
#                               127.0.0.1:4317 — verified on daniel-server, where it is the
#                               one port of this stack's seven already bound. Claude Code
#                               plugs into that collector instead; see claude-otel/README.md.
#   daniel-desktop              exports into daniel-wsl's collector over WSL2 localhost
#                               forwarding. A collector bound on the Windows side would
#                               shadow the very forward its telemetry travels through.
#   daniel-pi                   out of telemetry altogether (settings.base.json), so it has
#                               nothing to export and no reason to carry a stack.
#
# otelq is deliberately NOT ignored. It tries the node-local hostPort first and falls back to
# the cluster ClusterIPs (executable_otelq:59-61), so a query from daniel-box or daniel-server
# actually reaches that stack's Loki and Prometheus rather than querying an empty localhost —
# and the shape of the server stacks is changing anyway, so the fallback earns its keep.
{{ if has .chezmoi.hostname (list "daniel-server" "daniel-box" "daniel-desktop" "daniel-pi") }}
claude-otel
{{ end }}

# Warp tab configs split into two kinds, and only one kind is personal. The general machinery
# — settings.toml, keybindings.yaml, the Catppuccin theme, `local · shell` (which is also
# `default_tab_config_path`, so it has to exist everywhere), `local · chezmoi`, `local · vault`,
# and the free-text `ssh · other host` escape hatch — describes how Warp behaves and deploys
# to every machine.
#
# These three name personal infrastructure by hostname or by repo, and a work machine can
# reach none of them: `daniel-box` and `daniel-server` are ssh aliases for the homelab, and
# `~/server` is DanielH2018/server, the ansible repo that provisions it. Left ungated they are
# not merely dead entries — they put personal hostnames in the `+` menu of a work terminal.
#
# Gated on .work rather than on the hostname, unlike kwinrulesrc and fix-games-mount above:
# those are wrong on one specific machine's absence of hardware, this is wrong on a class of
# machine. A second personal box should get these without being added to a list.
#
# The list is spelled twice because Warp's data directory differs by OS, and only the spelling
# this machine actually deploys gates anything. A gate written in the other spelling matches no
# target, which chezmoi reports as no error at all — so a work laptop would get the personal
# hosts while the gate above it still looked correct.
{{ if .work }}
{{- if eq .chezmoi.os "windows" }}
AppData/Roaming/warp/Warp/data/tab_configs/daniel-box.toml
AppData/Roaming/warp/Warp/data/tab_configs/daniel-server.toml
AppData/Roaming/warp/Warp/data/tab_configs/local-server.toml
{{- else }}
.local/share/warp-terminal/tab_configs/daniel-box.toml
.local/share/warp-terminal/tab_configs/daniel-server.toml
.local/share/warp-terminal/tab_configs/local-server.toml
{{- end }}
{{ end }}

# Warp reads one settings.toml and one keybindings.yaml, at a path that differs by OS
# (docs.warp.dev/terminal/settings/file-locations). macOS reads ~/.warp; Linux reads
# $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/warp-terminal; Windows reads %LOCALAPPDATA%\warp\Warp\config. All three are
# tracked, all three include the same shared body from .chezmoitemplates, and each machine
# deploys only the pair its Warp actually opens — a copy at the other path is not harmless, it
# is a second file that looks authoritative and that a recapture can be pasted into with no
# effect.
#
# Themes and tab configs stay at the XDG data path on macOS and Linux, and macOS reaches them
# through two symlinks under ~/.warp. Warp only reads those two directories, so a symlink is
# safe there — unlike the pair above, which Warp rewrites.
#
# Windows cannot use that symlink bridge: creating one needs administrator privilege even with
# Developer Mode enabled, so it gets a real file tree under %APPDATA%\warp\Warp\data instead.
# Those files are one-line wrappers that `include` the XDG copy, which is raw and unrendered —
# so the content still has exactly one source and Warp's own `{{ "{{" }}host}}` placeholders
# survive. The cost is that the .work gate above is no longer a single copy of the personal-host
# list, which is why it is spelled twice and why the gate test resolves the spelling from the OS.
#
# Every path in this file is read as an entry, comments included, so no comment here may spell
# a real target path. That is what warp-tab-config-gate.test.js caught on the first draft.
{{ if eq .chezmoi.os "darwin" }}
.config/warp-terminal
AppData
{{ else if eq .chezmoi.os "windows" }}
.warp
.config/warp-terminal
.local/share/warp-terminal
{{ else }}
.warp
AppData
{{ if not (includeTemplate "is-desktop-linux" .) }}
# The user runs Warp on every desktop machine, never on daniel-box or daniel-server — headless
# hosts have no Warp session to read settings.toml/keybindings.yaml. Nested under the Linux
# branch above rather than folded into it, so darwin and windows keep their own unconditional
# paths untouched.
.config/warp-terminal
{{ end }}
{{ end }}

